furnishings for her country residence at Pavlovsk.According to a detailed description of her private rooms written in 1795 by Maria Feodorovna herself, the secretary was placed in her boudoir.[2] It remained at the imperial palace until the Soviet government, which had taken possession of Pavlovsk after the Revolution of 1917, offered works of art for sale to the dealer Joseph Duveen (1869–1939), who had traveled to the Soviet Union in 1931.[1] Campan 1823, vol. 1, p. 237.[2] Feodorovna 1795/1903, p. 374."@en ;
